==Old English==

===Etymology===
From earlier {{m|ang|æces}} (compare also Northumbrian {{m|ang|acas}}), from {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{m|gem-pro|*akwisī}}, probably from a {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{m|ine-pro|*h₂egʷs-ih₂-||axe}}, from {{m|ine-pro|*h₂eḱ-||sharp, pointed}}. Cognate with {{etyl|ofs|-}} {{m|ofs|axa}}, {{etyl|osx|-}} {{m|osx|akus}} ({{etyl|nds|-}} {{m|nds|Ax}}, {{m|nds|Äx}}, {{m|nds|Ex}}), {{etyl|nl|-}} {{m|nl|aaks}}, {{etyl|goh|-}} {{m|goh|ackus}} ({{etyl|de|-}} {{m|de|Axt}}), {{etyl|non|-}} {{m|non|øx}} ({{etyl|da|-}} {{m|da|økse}}, {{etyl|sv|-}} {{m|sv|yxa}}), {{etyl|got|-}} {{m|got|𐌰𐌵𐌹𐌶𐌹}}.

